  <!-- WHAT IS FABRIC SHRINKAGE -->
  <section class="shrinkage-section">
    <div class="shrinkage-container">
      <span class="section-label">Understanding Fabric Shrinkage</span>
      <h2 class="content-h2">What is Fabric Shrinkage and Why Does it Matter?</h2>
      <p class="content-prose">Fabric shrinkage is the reduction in a fabric's dimensions — length, width, or both — that occurs after washing, drying, or exposure to moisture and heat. For garment manufacturers, accurately predicting and accounting for shrinkage is one of the most critical steps in production. Failure to plan for shrinkage leads to finished garments that are smaller than specified, failing buyer measurements, causing costly returns, rejects, and repeat orders.</p>
      <p class="content-prose">Shrinkage occurs because textile fibers, yarns, and fabric structures are placed under tension during spinning, weaving, knitting, and finishing processes. When the fabric is washed, these internal stresses release, and the fibers return toward their natural, relaxed state — which is shorter. The higher the tension applied during manufacturing, and the more hygroscopic (moisture-absorbing) the fiber, the greater the shrinkage.</p>
      <p class="content-prose">For garment exporters in Bangladesh, India, and other major manufacturing countries, buyer specifications typically require total shrinkage to be within 3% to 5% for woven fabrics and 5% to 8% for knits after five wash cycles at the specified temperature. Exceeding these limits during pre-shipment testing can result in shipment holds, rejections, and financial penalties.</p>

      <h3 class="content-h3">The Four Types of Fabric Shrinkage</h3>
      <div class="info-grid">
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4 class="content-h4">Relaxation Shrinkage</h4>
          <p class="info-card-text">The most significant and irreversible type. Occurs during the first wash as fibers release mechanical stresses built up during production. Cotton, linen, and viscose are most affected. Relaxation shrinkage cannot be recovered — it is permanent from the first contact with water.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4 class="content-h4">Consolidation Shrinkage</h4>
          <p class="info-card-text">Occurs in knit fabrics when the looped yarn structure tightens after washing. The fabric consolidates into a denser, smaller structure. Knits always show higher total shrinkage than woven fabrics due to this mechanism, which is why knit buyers typically accept higher tolerances of 5% to 8%.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4 class="content-h4">Swelling Shrinkage</h4>
          <p class="info-card-text">Caused by fiber swelling when water molecules are absorbed. Cellulosic fibers like cotton and linen swell most, pushing neighboring fibers apart in one direction and compressing the fabric in the other. Higher wash temperatures amplify swelling shrinkage significantly.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4 class="content-h4">Felting Shrinkage</h4>
          <p class="info-card-text">Specific to wool and other animal hair fibers. The microscopic scales on wool fiber surfaces interlock during washing agitation and cannot be undone. Felting shrinkage is irreversible and can be extreme — untreated wool can shrink 30% or more in a single hot wash. Machine-washable wool receives anti-felting treatments to prevent this.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- HOW TO USE THE CALCULATOR -->
  <section class="shrinkage-section">
    <div class="shrinkage-container">
      <span class="section-label">Step-by-Step Guide</span>
      <h2 class="content-h2">How to Use This Shrinkage Calculator</h2>
      <p class="content-prose">This calculator uses fabric type, construction, GSM, wash conditions, and drying method to estimate shrinkage percentages based on industry-standard reference values. Here is what each input means and how the results should be interpreted.</p>

      <div class="method-grid">
        <div class="method-card">
          <span class="method-card-badge">Step 1</span>
          <h3 class="content-h3" style="margin-top: 0.5rem; font-size: 0.95rem;">Select Fabric Type and Construction</h3>
          <p class="method-card-text">Fiber content and fabric construction are the two strongest predictors of shrinkage. A 100% cotton knit will shrink considerably more than a polyester woven. Select the combination that best matches your fabric. If you have a blend, select the closest blend option.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="method-card">
          <span class="method-card-badge">Step 2</span>
          <h3 class="content-h3" style="margin-top: 0.5rem; font-size: 0.95rem;">Enter GSM and Yarn Count</h3>
          <p class="method-card-text">GSM (grams per square meter) indicates fabric weight. Heavier fabrics generally have more yarn mass and can store more stress, leading to slightly higher shrinkage. Yarn count helps refine the estimate for woven fabrics — finer yarns (higher Ne counts) tend to shrink less than coarser yarns.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="method-card">
          <span class="method-card-badge">Step 3</span>
          <h3 class="content-h3" style="margin-top: 0.5rem; font-size: 0.95rem;">Set Wash Temperature and Drying Method</h3>
          <p class="method-card-text">Use the wash temperature and drying method specified in the buyer's care label requirement or purchase order. For garment testing, the most common is 40°C machine wash with tumble dry or line dry. Higher temperatures dramatically increase natural fiber shrinkage. Always match test conditions to real-world consumer use.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="method-card">
          <span class="method-card-badge">Step 4</span>
          <h3 class="content-h3" style="margin-top: 0.5rem; font-size: 0.95rem;">Read Results and Apply to Patterns</h3>
          <p class="method-card-text">The calculator provides warp and weft shrinkage separately so you can apply the correct allowance to length and width independently. Add the pattern allowance values to your cut dimensions. For example, if warp shrinkage is 4.5% and you need a 70cm finished panel length, cut at 73.2cm. The buyer tolerance check tells you instantly whether your fabric meets requirements.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

