  <!-- SHRINKAGE CONTROL METHODS -->
  <section class="shrinkage-section">
    <div class="shrinkage-container">
      <span class="section-label">Production Guidance</span>
      <h2 class="content-h2">How to Control and Reduce Fabric Shrinkage</h2>
      <p class="content-prose">Understanding your fabric's shrinkage behavior is only the first step. In commercial garment production, multiple techniques are used to minimize shrinkage, make it predictable, and account for it accurately in pattern making. Here are the primary methods used in the industry.</p>

      <div class="info-grid">
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4 class="content-h4">Sanforizing</h4>
          <p class="info-card-text">A mechanical pre-shrinking process applied to woven cotton and denim fabrics before they leave the mill. The fabric is compressed between a rubber belt and a heated cylinder while moist, forcing controlled pre-shrinkage. Sanforized fabrics are certified to have residual shrinkage under 1%, making them highly stable for garment production. Look for the Sanforized trademark on fabric spec sheets when sourcing.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4 class="content-h4">Heat Setting (Synthetics)</h4>
          <p class="info-card-text">Polyester, nylon, and other thermoplastic fibers are heat-set at temperatures above their glass transition point during finishing. This locks the fiber molecules in a stable orientation, dramatically reducing heat-induced shrinkage in subsequent washing. Heat-set polyester fabrics typically show total shrinkage under 2% even at 60°C.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4 class="content-h4">Compressive Relaxation (Knits)</h4>
          <p class="info-card-text">Knit fabrics are passed through a compressive relaxation machine (or "compactor") that mechanically shortens the fabric while maintaining its structure. This pre-shrinks the knit, reducing post-wash shrinkage significantly. Compacted knits are essential for buyer tolerance compliance when producing T-shirts, polos, and underwear at scale.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4 class="content-h4">Pattern Cutting Allowance</h4>
          <p class="info-card-text">When pre-shrinking treatments are not available or complete, pattern cutting allowances compensate for expected shrinkage. Add the predicted shrinkage percentage to each cut dimension. Warp and weft allowances must be applied independently — garments shrink differently in length versus width. This calculator provides both values automatically.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4 class="content-h4">Fabric Pre-Washing</h4>
          <p class="info-card-text">For small-scale or specialty production where mechanical pre-shrinking is unavailable, the fabric can be pre-washed before cutting. This triggers most of the relaxation shrinkage before the garment is constructed. Pre-washing is labor-intensive and can affect fabric handle, but it is the most reliable way to remove shrinkage potential for certain natural fiber fabrics.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4 class="content-h4">Buyer Communication</h4>
          <p class="info-card-text">Always share shrinkage test reports with your buyer before bulk cutting. If your fabric's shrinkage exceeds buyer tolerance, negotiate either a pattern allowance agreement or alternative fabric sourcing. Surprises at pre-shipment inspection are far more costly than early disclosure. Document all shrinkage test results using the ISO 3759 labeling system for traceability.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bd-box">
        <h3 class="content-h3" style="margin-top: 0;">Fabric Shrinkage Testing at Bangladesh Garment Factories</h3>
        <p class="content-prose" style="max-width: 100%;">Bangladesh's garment sector produces over 40 billion USD worth of ready-made garments annually, and quality control — including shrinkage testing — is a contractual requirement for virtually every export order. International buyers and third-party labs (Bureau Veritas, SGS, Intertek, QIMA) routinely conduct fabric shrinkage tests at mills and garment factories in Dhaka, Gazipur, Narayanganj, and Chittagong before approving bulk fabric for production.</p>
        <p class="content-prose" style="max-width: 100%; margin-bottom: 0;">Most buyers specify testing to ISO 6330 or AATCC 135. Test samples are prepared per ISO 3759 (a minimum 50cm × 50cm marked area), washed at buyer-specified temperatures (commonly 40°C), dried by the specified method, and remeasured. Results must fall within the buyer's tolerance — typically 5% for woven cotton and 8% for cotton knits — before bulk fabric is released for cutting.</p>
        <div class="bd-note">
          SDF Clothing tests all incoming fabric for shrinkage compliance before cutting begins. Our quality team uses ISO 6330 protocols at 40°C with tumble dry to match the end-consumer use conditions for our export markets in the USA, UK, and EU. <a href="/quality-inspection-service/" style="color: #e00; text-decoration: none;">Learn more about our fabric testing process →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

